we begin in afghanistan where the  taliban says u.s and allied forces must  leave the country by the august 31st  deadline as agreed thousands of troops  are deployed in kabul where they're  carrying out the biggest humanitarian  airlift in history the evacuations have  been chaotic and there are reports that  unidentified gunmen have killed a man at  cowboy airport during a fire fight  involving western security forces well  u.s president joe biden says his forces  have expanded the perimeter around the  airport to accelerates the evacuation  process and while those evacuations are  underway the taliban says it has sent  fighters to panshire to counter  anti-taliban forces the group says it's  looking to negotiate rather than fights  well for more on this uh let's start at  the airport we can speak now to al  jazeera's charlotte bellis he joins us  on the phone  uh charlotte you're at the airport just  give us a sense of what the situation is  like right now  [Music]  right we're about 500 metres from the  north gate which is where everybody has  been asked or has been the last few days  been asked to go through uh thousands  thousands of people cramped  trying to get through but the gate is  fully shut there we have  uh western security forces  fence line  uh but there's really no movement it is  incredibly hot down here they are  dehydrated  um  where we've moved down to we're  essentially in the no man's land that's  being controlled by uh special  caa-funded  units that used to come under the afghan  intelligence school they have been  shooting in the air you just tear a  gunshot there  to disperse crowds and they are  essentially  controlling this area between the gate  that is shut  and the taliban and to get to this area  we had to move through taliban special  forces so  uh to give you a little bit of  appreciation of really how hard it is if  you have a  u.s citizenship passport any type of  western evacuees afghan evacuees to get  through this is the type of gordon you  have to run  and and charlotte we're also hearing  reports of a fire fight at the airport i  i appreciate you caught in the crush  right now and it's it sounds like a very  challenging situation uh to put it  mildly but have you and your team  learned anything more about that  firefight at the airport we understand  one person has been killed  we did ask about that at the north gate  where it was reported to have happened  uh people that have been here like  there's school pieces and things that  people have set up here they said there  was a big fire fight but it happened  late yesterday uh and that they know  that uh western forces were involved in  that uh we are some of these nds units  these afghan forces if they were  involved in the fight uh and they  directed us further down to the north  gate there's still a lot of confusion  but there's also a lot of uh gunfire  happening just as hard as crowd  dispersal uh they're also beating people  in front of us  uh it is uh anarchy and and chaotic and  there are children here there are women  here  uh and thousands of people  okay uh charlotte bellis uh we'll let  you go and and try and get to a place of  safety if one exists but thank you for  uh giving us a sense of the chaos and  anarchy  happening around kabul airport we  appreciate it speak soon  well let's uh cross over now to rob  mcbride he is live for us in kabul uh  rob we've been hearing about the ongoing  chaos there at the airport but uh  another interesting development that has  happened  in the last hour or so is that taliban  announcement that it's not going to  extend the deadline for western forces  to withdraw is rather interesting is it  not given a lot of the discussion  in uh  uh in the us and the uk is all about  trying to stay there as long as  necessary to get people out what more  are you hearing  yeah i mean it's interesting wording  about uh the the taliban giving  permission to extend i mean is it for  them to give permission i mean this  deadline has been something of a  moveable feast uh remember that may the  first was set as the first deadline for  uh u.s and nato forces to withdraw from  afghanistan that was agreed under the  peace deal with the taliban under the  trump administration last year now also  as part of that deal the taliban agreed  to talk to the ashraf ghani government  at the time well they didn't hold those  talks so when biden joe biden announced  in april he was extending the deadline  to the symbolically important september  11th they felt justified in doing so the  the thinking being well if the taliban  can break the deal we can break it too  so they biden the extent of the deadline  and then this august the 31st deadline  was again put in place by joe biden that  was put in place in july he brought  forward effectively his own deadline and  so he set it to august the 31st if he  set it then presumably he can unset it  which he seems to be doing now because  of what's going on at the airport also  at the urging of boris johnson of the uk  so  i what what the taliban will do it  remains unclear they have said they  won't allow an extension it's a red line  they have in the past warned that when  they extended the last time they were  going to target u.s  forces they didn't do that it remains  very doubtful that they would do it at  this stage because they have an awful  lot of other concerns rather than a  continuing scrap with the americans  they've got a country to run they've got  to get a country to get in order they've  got to deal with relations with their  neighbors try to unfreeze assets abroad  and everything else that running a  country involves so you do get a sense  that although they're going to talk  tough they probably it's in everybody's  interest that they do give the us the uk  and its allies  the extra days it needs to get the  people out and the taliban can get on  with running the country well they have  to get on with running the country as  you say rob but there's also uh  rumblings about uh a nascent resistance  uh movement uh what more can you tell us  about this  yeah this is in the panshir valley  that's north of kabul it's uh  it is the traditional home of resistance  to the taliban it was the home of the  northern alliance 20 years ago that was  the springboard for the u.s to intervene  after the twin towers came down and  sweep the taliban from power so it was  the natural place that a resistance  would form again and it's the son of  armacha massoud the lion of panchaya who  is something of a a hero especially  among the tajik community a resistance  fighter he has been in the panshir with  around 22 500  troops and also a couple of government  ministers from the collapsed regime but  it's it's difficult to say how serious a  resistance this is there is speculation  that uh massoud may have been in  negotiations to uh to  basically put down their arms and to  support the government that seems to  have gone away there might be some  resistance but we do know that the  taliban themselves are taking this  seriously and they're pretty much  surrounded the valley with uh superior  forces so it is something of a standoff  but it remains to be seeing if the  resistance has the force  to survive this okay rob mcbride there  joining us live from kabul with the  latest developments thank you rob
 

